Port `src/indexer/discover.ts` (393 lines).
```rust
pub const ALWAYS_EXCLUDED: [&str; 5] = [".git", "node_modules", ".sangkar", "dist", "vendor"];
pub const SIZE_CAP_BYTES: u64 = 512 * 1024 * 1024;
pub enum SkipReason { Ignored, Binary, SizeCap, UnsupportedExt, Unreadable }
pub struct DiscoveredFile { pub path: String, pub lang: LangId, pub size: u64, pub sha256: String }
pub struct DiscoverResult { pub files: Vec<DiscoveredFile>, pub not_indexed: Vec<NotIndexedFile>, pub skipped: usize }
pub fn discover(repo_root: &Path, opts: &DiscoverOptions) -> DiscoverResult;
```
Rules that must survive: ignore rules come from `<root>/.gitignore` then `<root>/.sangkarignore` with the documented glob subset (`*`, `?` stay in a segment; `**` crosses; a leading `/` anchors; a trailing `/` is a directory; `#` comments; `!` negates, last match wins; no character classes, no escapes); the two control files themselves are neither indexed nor reported; symlinks are never followed; classification order is ignore -> extension -> size -> binary probe (first 8192 bytes containing a NUL) -> sha256, with `HASH_CHUNK_BYTES = 1 MiB`; an unreadable directory is recorded as reason `unreadable` (EACCES/EPERM/ENOENT) instead of aborting the run; output order is byte-wise string order of the path, no locale.
Tests port `tests/discover.test.ts`: each of the five skip reasons, a nested `.gitignore`-style rule set from the root control files, a symlink loop, an unreadable directory (created with `chmod 000` at test time and restored), the size cap via a sparse file, and the `src-old` sibling behaviour.

Port `src/indexer/parse.ts` (391 lines), minus the WASM loader it exists to work around.
```rust
pub const MAX_CACHED_LANGUAGES: usize = 6;
pub const MAX_ERROR_RANGES: usize = 20;
pub enum ParseFailureReason { GrammarUnavailable, ParseFailed }
pub enum ParseResult { Ok { tree: tree_sitter::Tree, error_ranges: Vec<String> },
                        Err { reason: ParseFailureReason, detail: String } }
pub fn parse_file(source: &[u8], lang: LangId) -> ParseResult;    // never panics, never returns Err for a syntax error
```
An LRU of `(LangId, Parser)` with capacity 6, hit = move to most-recently-used, eviction of the least-recently-used beyond the cap. `error_ranges` collects every `ERROR` or `MISSING` node as `"12"` or `"12-14"` (1-based lines), deduplicated in tree order, capped at 20 with a final `"+N more ranges"` entry.
The Rust version may parse concurrently; keep a `Mutex`-guarded pool and make the public function `&self` so the worker can decide the threading. Do not add a language-inspection or syntax-highlighting API — the extractor needs the tree only.
Tests: a well-formed file per language (parse ok, no error ranges), a file with a deliberate syntax error (ok with at least one range and the `"a-b"` form), a file with 25+ errors (cap plus the `+N more` tail), and grammar-unavailable behaviour when asked for a language without a compiled grammar (assert the variant, not a panic).

This is the first half of `src/indexer/extract.ts` (946 lines); the second half is TASK-042 and both own `extract/`. Keep the split at `declare.rs` (declarations, containment, imports) vs `sites.rs` (calls, inheritance, references) and let `mod.rs` hold only the shared types and the single walk that calls both.
Shared types (both halves obey them):
```rust
pub const NODE_KINDS: [&str; 7] = ["file","module","class","function","method","test","endpoint"];
pub const RAW_EDGE_KINDS: [&str; 6] = ["contains","calls","imports","inherits","implements","references"];
pub const EXTRACTED_CANDIDATE: &str = "extracted_candidate";
pub const REFERENCE_SITES: [&str; 6] = ["call_argument","assignment_rhs","object_element","array_element","decorator_argument","return_expression"];
pub struct ExtractedNode { pub kind: String, pub name: String, pub qn: String, pub start_line: u32, pub end_line: u32,
                            pub signature: Option<String>, pub is_test: bool, pub props: NodeProps }
pub struct RawEdge { pub kind: String, pub src: String, pub target: String, pub receiver: Option<String>,
                     pub line: u32, pub tier: String, pub evidence: Option<String>, pub local_name: String }
pub struct UnresolvedSite { pub line: u32, pub raw_target: String, pub evidence_kind: String, pub evidence: String }
pub struct ExtractResult { pub nodes: Vec<ExtractedNode>, pub raw_edges: Vec<RawEdge>, pub unresolved: Vec<UnresolvedSite> }
```
`declare.rs` must emit: the `file` node for the path, a `module` node where the language has one (the `fileModule` rule for Python), every class node from `class_types`, every function/method from `function_types` (a function inside a class is `method`), `is_test` from the row's `test_file_regex` on the path AND from the language's test-decorator convention, `signature` as the source text of the declaration header truncated the way the TypeScript side truncates it, and `contains` edges parent->child. Imports come from `import_types`: one `imports` raw edge per imported binding with `local_name` and the specifier as `target`.
Qualified names use `paths::qn(rel_path, scope, name)` exactly, because `node.qn` is UNIQUE and every later binding step depends on the shape.
Tests: one snippet per language family (python, typescript, java, go, rust, ruby) asserting node kinds, qn shape, containment edges, `is_test`, and the import edges with their `local_name`.

Second half of `src/indexer/persist.ts`. Constants: `NEIGHBOURHOOD_LIMIT = 5_000`, `ID_BATCH = 400`, `FTS_REBUILD_FRACTION = 0.35`, `MIRROR_COLUMNS = "rowid_, name, qn, signature, docstring, props_text"`, meta keys `generation`, `generation_seq`, `project_name`, `project_root`, `project_branch`, `project_remote`.
```rust
pub struct GenerationInput { pub files_indexed: i64, pub nodes: i64, pub edges: i64, pub duration_ms: i64,
                             pub mode: String, pub base_commit: Option<String>, pub worktree_dirty: bool,
                             pub identity: ProjectIdentity }
pub struct GenerationResult { pub generation: String, pub seq: i64, pub fts: FtsReport, pub metrics: MetricsReport }
pub fn finalize_generation(tx: &Transaction, input: &GenerationInput) -> Result<GenerationResult>;
```
The derivations, in the documented order: degree update (`INSERT OR IGNORE INTO node_metrics (node_id) SELECT id FROM node`, then the two correlated `UPDATE node_metrics SET in_degree = (SELECT count(*) ... )` statements); the `tested_by` rebuild (`DELETE FROM edge WHERE kind = 'tested_by'`, then the `INSERT INTO edge ... SELECT ... GROUP BY c.src_id, c.dst_id ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` over test->non-test calls); `tested_depth` (NULL, then 1 for direct, then 2 for one hop); the tombstone settle (`UPDATE tombstone SET removed_in_generation = ?`, delete settled ones whose qn came back); entry points (`reachable_count` from the `NEIGHBOURHOOD_LIMIT`-bounded neighbourhood, batched by `ID_BATCH`); the FTS mirror delta (rows in `node_fts_state` with no node -> delete from the virtual table with the `('delete', rowid, ...)` form and from the mirror; rows in `node` with no mirror row -> insert into both) with a full `INSERT INTO node_fts (node_fts) VALUES ('rebuild')` when the delta exceeds 35 % of nodes; then the generation row (`seq = coalesce(max(seq),0)+1`, previous `published` rows set to `superseded`, new row `published`) and the six meta upserts.
Tests: a graph with a test calling a production function (tested_depth 1 and 2), a node with no metrics row, a removed symbol settling its tombstone, an FTS delta above and below the rebuild threshold, and the generation sequence after three publishes.

Port `src/indexer/watch.ts` (671 lines).
```rust
pub const DEFAULT_INTERVAL_MS: u64 = 2_000;
pub const DEFAULT_DEBOUNCE_MS: u64 = 500;
pub const MAX_BACKOFF_MS: u64 = 300_000;
pub fn backoff_ms(interval_ms: u64, failures: u32) -> u64;   // min(interval * 2^failures, 300_000)
pub struct WatchHandle { /* stop(), stats() */ }
pub fn start_watch(opts: &WatchOptions) -> WatchHandle;
```
Detector: `git` mode reads `git rev-parse --git-dir --show-prefix`, `git rev-parse HEAD`, `git status --porcelain -z -uall` with the same prologue (`--no-pager --no-optional-locks -c credential.helper= -c core.askpass= -C <root>`) and a 10 s timeout; the signature is `git\n<HEAD>\n<porcelain -z -uall>\n<stamp>` where the stamp is `rel\0floor(mtime_ms)\0size` per parseable dirty/untracked path. `mtime` mode is the fallback: `mtime\n<count>\n<sha256 of sorted rows>`. The FIRST poll only records a baseline and never spawns.
Debounce: on a changed signature, sleep `DEBOUNCE_MS` once, re-observe once, and spawn only if still different (no window restart). Loop delay = `interval` when `failures == 0`, else the backoff. Exit codes: `0`/`2` advance the baseline, `75` is logged and not a failure, anything else increments `failures`.
Spawn target: `SpawnWorker` closure so tests inject a fake; production spawns `<config.worker_cmd> worker --repo-root <root> --data-dir <dir>` with stderr to the daemon log and reports only the exit status.
Tests port `tests/watch.test.ts`: baseline-only first poll, a real file change spawning once, a two-change burst spawning once (debounce), a worker exiting 1 raising the backoff and 75 not raising it, `backoff_ms` at failures 0..6, and the mtime detector on a directory without git.
